<subsection> 
 <title>Formulas for correlation and regression</title>
<p>
Correlation:
<ul>
<li><m> (x_{i}, y_{i}) </m> are the paired data.</li>
<li><m>n</m> is the sample size.</li>
<li><m>\overline{x}</m> and <m>\overline{y}</m> are the sample means for the two variables.</li>
<li><m>s_x</m> and <m> s_y </m> are the sample standard deviations for the variables.</li>
<li><m>\displaystyle r =  \frac{1}{n-1} \sum \left( \frac{x_i - \overline{x}}{s_x}  \right) \left( \frac{y_i - \overline{y}}{s_y}  \right)  </m> is the pearson correlation coefficient.</li>
<li><m>\rho </m> is the population correlation coefficient.</li>
<li><m>\displaystyle \text{SE}_r =  \sqrt{\frac{1-r^2}{n-2} }  </m> is the theory-based standard error for correlation.</li>
<li><m>t = r/SE_r  </m> is the standardized statistic for correlation.</li>
</ul>
</p>
<p>
Regression:
<ul>
<li>
  <p>
    <m>\hat{y} = a + b \cdot x</m> is the least squares regression equation that predicts the value
    <m>\hat{y}_i </m> for the value <m> x_i </m></li>
    <ul>
      <li><m>a = \overline{y} - b \cdot \overline{x}  </m> is the intercept of the regression equation that represents the predicted value <m> \hat{y}</m> when <m>x = 0 </m>.</li>
      <li><m>\displaystyle b = r \frac{s_y}{s_x} </m> is the slope of the regression equation that represents how much the predicted value <m> \hat{y}</m> increases/decreases per a change of 1 unit of <m> x </m>.</li>
      </ul>
      <li><m> \text{SAE} = \sum |y_i - \overline{y}| </m> is the sum of absolute errors.</li>
      <li><m> \text{SSE} </m> is the sum of square residuals (sum of square errors).</li>
      <ul>
  </p>
<li><m>\text{SSE}(\overline{x}) = \sum (x_i - \overline{x} )^2 </m> is the sum of square residuals for <m>x</m>.</li>
<li><m> \text{SSE}(\overline{y}) = \sum (y_i - \overline{y} )^2 </m> is the sum of square residuals for <m>y</m>.</li>
<li><m> \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line}) = \sum (y_i - \hat{y}_i )^2 </m> is the sum of square residuals for the regression line.</li>
</ul>
<li><m>R^2 = \frac{\text{SSE}(\overline{y}) - \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line}) }{\text{SSE}(\overline{y})} =  r^2 </m> is the coefficient of determination that measures the proportion of total variation in <m>y</m> that is explained by the linear relationship with <m>x</m>.</li>
<li><m>\text{SE}</m> is standard error.</li>
<ul>
<li><m>\displaystyle \text{SE}_a =  \sqrt{ \left( \frac{ \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line})  }{n-2} \right) \left( \frac{1}{n} + \frac{\overline{x}^2}{\text{SSE}(\overline{x}) } \right)  }   </m> is the theory-based standard error for intercept.</li>
<li><m>\displaystyle \text{SE}_b =  \frac{1}{\sqrt{n-2}} \sqrt{ \frac{ \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line}) }{\text{SSE}(\overline{x}) }  }  </m> is the theory-based standard error for slope.</li>
</ul>
<li><m>t </m> is standardized statistic .</li>
<ul>
<li><m>t = b/SE_b  </m> is the standardized statistic for slope.</li>
<li><m>t = a/SE_a  </m> is the standardized statistic for intercept.</li>
</ul>
<li><m> \beta </m> is the population regression slope that represents how much <m>y</m> changes on average per a change of 1 unit in <m>x</m>.</li>
<li><m> b \pm (\text{Multiplier}) \cdot SE_b </m> is the theory-based is a theory-based confidence interval estimating <m>\beta</m>, where the multiplier comes from a t distribution with <m> n-2</m> degrees of freedom. </li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><m>a = \overline{y} - b \cdot \overline{x}  </m> is the intercept of the regression equation that represents the predicted value <m> \hat{y}</m> when <m>x = 0 </m>.</li>
<li><m>\displaystyle b = r \frac{s_y}{s_x} </m> is the slope of the regression equation that represents how much the predicted value <m> \hat{y}</m> increases/decreases per a change of 1 unit of <m> x </m>.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\text{SSE}(\overline{x}) = \sum (x_i - \overline{x} )^2 </m> is the sum of square residuals for <m>x</m>.</li>
<li><m> \text{SSE}(\overline{y}) = \sum (y_i - \overline{y} )^2 </m> is the sum of square residuals for <m>y</m>.</li>
<li><m> \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line}) = \sum (y_i - \hat{y}_i )^2 </m> is the sum of square residuals for the regression line.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><m>\displaystyle \text{SE}_a =  \sqrt{ \left( \frac{ \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line})  }{n-2} \right) \left( \frac{1}{n} + \frac{\overline{x}^2}{\text{SSE}(\overline{x}) } \right)  }   </m> is the theory-based standard error for intercept.</li>
<li><m>\displaystyle \text{SE}_b =  \frac{1}{\sqrt{n-2}} \sqrt{ \frac{ \text{SSE}(\text{Regression Line}) }{\text{SSE}(\overline{x}) }  }  </m> is the theory-based standard error for slope.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><m>t = b/SE_b  </m> is the standardized statistic for slope.</li>
<li><m>t = a/SE_a  </m> is the standardized statistic for intercept.</li>
</ul>
</p>
</subsection>

